DETAILED AGENT PROMPT — Hide Dropdown Arrow After Selection (More Text Visible)

Goal
After a product is selected, hide or minimize the native dropdown arrow so more of the product name is visible, without changing any other behavior, layout, or calculations. The selection must remain editable (user can still open the dropdown to change).

Constraints (Must Not Change)
- Do NOT change table column widths or layout.
- Do NOT replace the <select> with a different control.
- Do NOT break accessibility or keyboard navigation.
- Do NOT alter calculation logic or any other UI.

Preferred Approach (CSS-only, safe)
Use a class on the select once it has a value, and hide the native arrow while keeping the control functional.

Implementation Steps
1) On product selection change:
	- If a valid product is selected, add a class: .selected
	- If empty/unselected, remove the class.

2) CSS to hide the arrow (cross‑browser best effort):
	- For modern browsers: appearance: none; background-image: none;
	- For IE/Edge legacy: select::-ms-expand { display: none; }
	- Keep padding-right minimal so text has more space.

3) Maintain click behavior:
	- Do NOT set pointer-events: none.
	- Keep the select focusable.

Example CSS (scope to Product column only)
- #invTable td:first-child select.selected {
	 appearance: none;
	 -webkit-appearance: none;
	 -moz-appearance: none;
	 background-image: none;
	 padding-right: 4px; /* reduce reserved space */
  }
- #invTable td:first-child select.selected::-ms-expand { display: none; }

Example JS Hook
- On selection change:
  if (this.value) this.classList.add('selected');
  else this.classList.remove('selected');

Acceptance Criteria
- After a product is selected, the dropdown arrow is hidden or minimized.
- More product name text is visible within the same column width.
- Dropdown still opens and works on click/tap.
- No changes to other columns, layouts, or calculations.

